### AgentCore Gateway, spoken to directly — no agent in the path
#
# This is the file that answers "who actually said no". Every request here is plain JSON-RPC
# 2.0 over HTTP to the Gateway's `/mcp` endpoint, with a Cognito token as the bearer. There
# is no model call and no container of ours involved, so it costs nothing and any refusal is
# demonstrably AgentCore's decision rather than our code's.
#
# A TEMPLATE — generate the runnable copy with `./scripts/http-requests.sh`.
# Send `token` first, then `initialize`, then the rest.

@tokenEndpoint = __TOKEN_ENDPOINT__
@basicAuth = __BASIC_AUTH__
@gatewayUrl = __GATEWAY_URL__

### token WITHOUT the photos scope — so one tool is allowed and one is not
# @name token
POST {{tokenEndpoint}}
Authorization: Basic {{basicAuth}}
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded

grant_type=client_credentials&scope=tools/weather:read tools/places:read

### initialize — the MCP handshake
# The Gateway supports exactly one protocol version, `2025-03-26`, and says so usefully if you
# send another. Note what comes back in `result.protocolVersion`: a client should adopt that
# answer rather than keep announcing its own preference. Ours does; the first smoke script
# pinned a version and broke on first contact.
POST {{gatewayUrl}}
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}
Content-Type: application/json
Accept: application/json, text/event-stream
Mcp-Protocol-Version: 2025-03-26
x-travel-session-id: http-file-session

{
  "jsonrpc": "2.0",
  "id": 1,
  "method": "initialize",
  "params": {
    "protocolVersion": "2025-03-26",
    "capabilities": {},
    "clientInfo": { "name": "http-file", "version": "1.0" }
  }
}

### tools/list — what the model would be told it can do
# Three tools, each named `travel-tools___<tool>`: AgentCore prefixes every tool with its
# target's name, and stripping that prefix is the client's job (src/gateway/naming.ts).
#
# Note what is NOT happening here: the list is not filtered by scope. Hiding a tool the caller
# may not use would remove the denial trace the observability requirement is built on
# (ADR-0004), so the catalogue is the same for everyone and the refusal happens at call time.
POST {{gatewayUrl}}
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}
Content-Type: application/json
Accept: application/json, text/event-stream
Mcp-Protocol-Version: 2025-03-26
x-travel-session-id: http-file-session

{ "jsonrpc": "2.0", "id": 2, "method": "tools/list", "params": {} }

### tools/call get_weather — scope granted, expect a real forecast
# The `weekday` field is computed by the tool, not by the model. The model was off by one day
# when it derived weekdays itself, which is why that field exists.
POST {{gatewayUrl}}
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}
Content-Type: application/json
Accept: application/json, text/event-stream
Mcp-Protocol-Version: 2025-03-26
x-travel-session-id: http-file-session

{
  "jsonrpc": "2.0",
  "id": 3,
  "method": "tools/call",
  "params": { "name": "travel-tools___get_weather", "arguments": { "city": "Lisbon" } }
}

### tools/call get_photos — scope NOT granted, expect blocked: true
# THE experiment. `{"error":"Missing scope \"photos:search\"...","blocked":true}` with
# `isError: true`, produced by the REQUEST interceptor before the target Lambda ran at all.
# Before the Gateway existed, the same refusal was produced by our own code, inside the
# component being restricted — which is not a control, it is a promise.
#
# Then look at /aws/lambda/travel-assistant-gateway-interceptor for a `gateway.authorize` span
# with status `blocked`, carrying `x-travel-session-id` above as its sessionId.
POST {{gatewayUrl}}
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}
Content-Type: application/json
Accept: application/json, text/event-stream
Mcp-Protocol-Version: 2025-03-26
x-travel-session-id: http-file-session

{
  "jsonrpc": "2.0",
  "id": 4,
  "method": "tools/call",
  "params": { "name": "travel-tools___get_photos", "arguments": { "place": "Lisbon" } }
}

### tools/call an unknown tool — expect a refusal too, because the interceptor fails closed
# A tool the Gateway serves but our interceptor does not know cannot have its required scope
# looked up, and "unknown, therefore allowed" is how a target added without a matching code
# change ends up with no authorization at all.
POST {{gatewayUrl}}
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}
Content-Type: application/json
Accept: application/json, text/event-stream
Mcp-Protocol-Version: 2025-03-26
x-travel-session-id: http-file-session

{
  "jsonrpc": "2.0",
  "id": 5,
  "method": "tools/call",
  "params": { "name": "travel-tools___drop_database", "arguments": {} }
}

### a deliberately wrong protocol version — the Gateway tells you what it supports
# Expect `{"code":-32600,"message":"Unsupported protocol version: 2025-06-18", ...}` naming
# `["2025-03-26"]`. Worth sending once: this is the error our client survives by negotiating.
POST {{gatewayUrl}}
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}
Content-Type: application/json
Accept: application/json, text/event-stream
Mcp-Protocol-Version: 2025-06-18
x-travel-session-id: http-file-session

{ "jsonrpc": "2.0", "id": 6, "method": "tools/list", "params": {} }
